{# Document header: a two-column grid. Identity sits left, context right, and
   the byline spans both beneath them, so the width earns its keep instead of
   stacking five short lines down the page.

   Source order is identity, byline, context -- deliberately not the visual
   order. Grid placement puts context back up on row one, which lets the author
   follow the subtitle for a screen reader and for pdftotext without moving
   anything on screen. On a narrow viewport the columns collapse and the header
   stacks in this source order -- see _page-style.css.j2.

   `has-context`, `has-byline` and `has-dates` come from
   frontmatter-filter.lua, which is also what guarantees a blank key reads as
   an absent one here. #}
$if(title)$
<header class="doc-title">
    <div class="doc-identity">
      {# An h1, not bare text in a div. A document had no h1 at ALL: its first
         heading was whatever the author wrote, normally ##, so every document
         opened on an H2 with nothing above it. PDF/UA 7.4.2 rejects that --
         "Heading level 1 is skipped in a descending sequence" -- and it is
         wrong on its own terms, since the document's title IS its top heading.
         The deck has always spelled this <h1 class="deck-title">.

         Styling stays on .doc-title, the header, and .doc-name only cancels
         the user-agent h1 so nothing moves: the heading inherits the size,
         weight and family it was already rendering at. #}
      <h1 class="doc-name">$title$</h1>
$if(subtitle)$
      <div class="doc-subtitle">$subtitle$</div>
$endif$
    </div>
$if(has-byline)$
  <div class="doc-byline">
$if(author)$
    {# Each name is its own element so a list wraps between names rather
       than mid-name, and the separator travels with the name it follows.
       author: takes a single value or a YAML list; a list is what a team
       project needs, and six names have to survive the header.

       Every gap here is a character, not a flex gap. A gap paints the space
       and leaves nothing behind, so copy-paste and BeautifulSoup both read
       "AuthorAda Lovelace". That was 0.11.0, and it fixed the HTML.

       It did not fix the PDF, and this is the measured reason (stn-40n): a
       whitespace-only text node sitting between two inline boxes never
       reaches the PDF text layer. Chromium paints the advance and emits no
       glyph, so `Author</span> <span>` extracted as "AuthorAda Lovelace"
       from a page whose DOM had the space. Substituting &#160; there changes
       nothing -- a lone nbsp between two boxes is dropped exactly the same
       way. What survives is a space inside a text run that also carries a
       printing character, which is why .doc-fact-sep's `&#160;&middot;&#160;`
       already came through and why `Points&#160;50 pts` did.

       So the gap lives INSIDE the label span and INSIDE the separator span,
       never in a text node between them. Measure it in the PDF, not the DOM:
       tests/test_pdf.py holds this, and the DOM-level test in test_dates.py
       passed throughout the whole time this was broken.

       <wbr> gives back what the nbsp pair takes away. Glued on both sides the
       separator makes the author list one unbreakable run, and six names then
       overflow the header instead of wrapping between names. <wbr> is a break
       opportunity rather than a character, so it costs the text layer nothing.
       Measured in the browser image: six names still break between names, on
       screen and in print media, and no name splits across lines.

       The space before the separator belongs to the NAME, not to the
       separator, and that is deliberate. The separator is aria-hidden, which
       removes its whole subtree from the accessibility tree -- so a space
       parked inside it reaches the PDF but not a screen reader, which is this
       same bug wearing a different coat. Chromium's accessibility tree for
       the 0.12.0 markup read 'Author', 'Ada Lovelace', 'Grace Hopper' as
       adjacent runs with no whitespace anywhere between them; with the space
       inside the name it reads 'Ada Lovelace ', 'Grace Hopper '. The
       trailing space on the last name costs a few pixels at the end of a
       left-aligned line and nothing else. #}
    <div class="doc-meta"><span class="doc-label">Author&#160;</span>$for(author)$<span class="doc-author">$author$&#160;</span>$sep$<span class="doc-author-sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;&#160;</span><wbr>$endfor$</div>
$endif$
$if(has-facts)$
    {# Issued, Due and Points on one line, spread across the space the author
       does not use. They were a stacked column and a badge beside the title;
       the badge read as a UI chip on a document that has no other chrome.

       Separators are their own elements rather than ::before content, so the
       flex row can space them evenly instead of hanging each one off the item
       that follows it. Each is conditional on something actually preceding
       it AND on the item it precedes existing -- a separator emitted on
       'something came before' alone strands one whenever the next fact is
       absent, which is the bug this shape prevents.

       The separator's spaces are non-breaking and sit inside the separator's
       own span. It is its own flex item, so ordinary spaces collapse away at
       its edges and the header extracts as "Sep 01·Due" -- fine on screen,
       wrong in a paste and wrong in pdftotext. Each label's gap is inside the
       label span for the same reason, one step further: see .doc-meta above
       for what the PDF text layer does and does not keep.

       One line on purpose: a newline between these collapses to a word space
       that lands on one side of a separator only. #}
    <div class="doc-facts">$if(date-label)$<span class="doc-fact doc-issued"><span class="doc-label">Issued&#160;</span><time datetime="$date-iso$">$date-label$</time></span>$endif$$if(due-label)$$if(sep-before-due)$<span class="doc-fact-sep" aria-hidden="true">&#160;&middot;&#160;</span>$endif$<span class="doc-fact doc-due"><span class="doc-label">Due&#160;</span><time datetime="$due-iso$">$due-label$</time></span>$endif$$if(points-label)$$if(sep-before-points)$<span class="doc-fact-sep" aria-hidden="true">&#160;&middot;&#160;</span>$endif$<span class="doc-fact doc-points"><span class="doc-label">Points&#160;</span>$points-label$</span>$endif$</div>
$endif$
  </div>
$endif$
$if(has-context)$
  {# Written last, placed first. One line on purpose: a newline between these
     spans collapses to a word space that lands on only one side of the
     generated separator, which is asymmetric in a way no margin can correct. #}
  <div class="doc-context">$if(brand)$<div class="doc-brand$if(brand-is-image)$ doc-brand--image$endif$">$brand$</div>$endif$$if(program)$<span class="doc-program">$program$</span>$endif$$if(section)$<span class="doc-section">$section$</span>$endif$$if(term)$<span class="doc-term">$term$</span>$endif$</div>
$endif$
</header>
$endif$
$body$
